from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path

g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']
def P(*a): return L(*a,language='python')

cv=[
('An image is a number array','OpenCV is a library for working with images. A small colour image can be stored as rows, columns and three colour values per pixel.','image = np.zeros((3, 4, 3), dtype=np.uint8)\nprint(image.shape)','The output is (3, 4, 3). There are three rows, four columns and three colour channels. zeros makes a black image with every value equal to zero.','Create an image with two rows and five columns.','Array shape lists height before width; mixing them up swaps the intended picture size.','Which number gives the width here?','4','3'),
('Know the BGR channel order','OpenCV commonly uses blue, green, red order for colour images. Each uint8 channel can hold a whole number from 0 to 255.','image = np.zeros((1, 1, 3), dtype=np.uint8)\nimage[0, 0] = [255, 0, 0]\nprint(image[0, 0].tolist())','The output is [255, 0, 0]. In OpenCV BGR order, this pixel is blue because the first channel is at its maximum.','Make the pixel red by moving 255 to the right channel.','Many other image tools use RGB order; check the order before passing images between tools.','What colour is this BGR pixel?','Blue','Red'),
('Convert BGR to RGB','A colour conversion can reorder channels so another tool reads them correctly. COLOR_BGR2RGB swaps the blue and red positions.','bgr = np.array([[[255, 0, 0]]], dtype=np.uint8)\nrgb = cv2.cvtColor(bgr, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\nprint(rgb[0, 0].tolist())','The output is [0, 0, 255]. It is still the same blue colour, now described in RGB order.','Convert a red BGR pixel and check its RGB values.','Reordering channels changes the representation; it does not mean the visible colour should change.','What does BGR2RGB change?','Channel order','The image width'),
('Make a grayscale image','Grayscale stores one brightness value per pixel. It drops colour detail while keeping a brightness view useful for some image operations.','bgr = np.full((2, 3, 3), 100, dtype=np.uint8)\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(bgr, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nprint(gray.shape, gray[0, 0].item())','The output is (2, 3) 100. Equal BGR values of 100 become a gray value of 100. The result has no three-channel dimension.','Use equal channel values of 200.','Grayscale conversion is not simply taking the blue channel for every coloured pixel.','How many channel values per pixel are in gray?','1','3'),
('Crop by rows and columns','Cropping selects a rectangle from an image. NumPy slices use start positions and stop just before the end positions.','image = np.arange(20, dtype=np.uint8).reshape(4, 5)\ncrop = image[1:3, 2:5].copy()\nprint(crop.tolist())','The output is [[7, 8, 9], [12, 13, 14]]. Rows 1 and 2 and columns 2, 3 and 4 are selected. copy makes a separate array.','Crop the first two rows and first two columns.','A slice can share memory with its source; use copy when you need independent pixels.','Is row 3 included in the slice 1:3?','No','Yes'),
('Resize to a chosen size','Resizing changes image dimensions. OpenCV resize takes the target size as width, height, unlike the row-first array shape.','image = np.zeros((4, 6, 3), dtype=np.uint8)\nsmall = cv2.resize(image, (3, 2), interpolation=cv2.INTER_NEAREST)\nprint(small.shape)','The output is (2, 3, 3). The requested width is 3 and height is 2. Nearest-neighbour resizing selects nearby pixel values.','Resize to width 2 and height 4, then predict the shape.','Changing width and height by different factors can stretch the picture.','What order does resize use for its size tuple?','Width, height','Height, width'),
('Threshold brightness','Thresholding turns brightness values into two groups. A binary threshold sets values above the limit to a maximum and the rest to zero.','gray = np.array([[50, 100, 150]], dtype=np.uint8)\n_, binary = cv2.threshold(gray, 100, 255, cv2.THRESH_BINARY)\nprint(binary.tolist())','The output is [[0, 0, 255]]. Only 150 is greater than 100. A value equal to the threshold stays zero for this mode.','Lower the threshold to 99 and predict which values become 255.','The exact comparison depends on the selected threshold mode.','What happens to the value exactly 100 here?','It becomes 0','It becomes 255'),
('Blur nearby pixels','Blurring combines neighbouring pixel values to soften local changes. A kernel is the small neighbourhood used for the calculation.','image = np.zeros((5, 5), dtype=np.uint8)\nimage[2, 2] = 255\nblurred = cv2.GaussianBlur(image, (3, 3), 0)\nprint(int(blurred[2, 2]) < 255, int(blurred[2, 1]) > 0)','The output is True True. Some brightness spreads from the centre to nearby pixels, so the centre becomes dimmer and its neighbour becomes brighter.','Try a 5 by 5 kernel and inspect how far the brightness spreads.','A blur can hide useful detail as well as noise.','What does the kernel size describe?','The local neighbourhood size','The number of saved files'),
('Find candidate edges','An edge is a strong image change, often in brightness. Canny finds candidate edges after several processing steps; an edge is not an object label.','image = np.zeros((20, 20), dtype=np.uint8)\nimage[5:15, 5:15] = 255\nedges = cv2.Canny(image, 50, 150)\nprint(edges.shape, bool(np.any(edges)))','The output is (20, 20) True. The result keeps the image size and contains nonzero edge pixels around the bright square. It does not name the shape for us.','Move the square by changing both slice ranges.','Edge detection does not understand what an object is or whether it is safe.','Does Canny label the object as a square?','No','Yes'),
('Draw with image coordinates','OpenCV drawing points use x, y order. x is the column and y is the row. A negative thickness can fill a shape.','image = np.zeros((5, 5, 3), dtype=np.uint8)\ncv2.rectangle(image, (1, 1), (3, 3), (0, 255, 0), thickness=-1)\nprint(image[2, 2].tolist(), image[0, 0].tolist())','The centre pixel is [0, 255, 0], which is green in BGR. The corner pixel remains [0, 0, 0]. The rectangle fills the selected area.','Draw the rectangle in blue instead.','Array indexing is row, column, but drawing points are x, y.','Which point coordinate names the column?','x','y'),
('Use a mask to select pixels','A mask is an image that marks which pixels to include. In this operation, a nonzero mask value allows the source pixel through.','image = np.array([[10, 20], [30, 40]], dtype=np.uint8)\nmask = np.array([[255, 0], [0, 255]], dtype=np.uint8)\nselected = cv2.bitwise_and(image, image, mask=mask)\nprint(selected.tolist())','The output is [[10, 0], [0, 40]]. The mask keeps the two diagonal pixels and blocks the other two.','Change the mask to keep only the top row.','The mask needs matching image width and height and a suitable single-channel type.','Which value is kept at the bottom right?','40','0'),
('Save a small local picture','imwrite saves image pixels to a file. Check its return value and choose a practice path so you do not overwrite work you need.','image = np.full((10, 10, 3), [0, 255, 0], dtype=np.uint8)\nsaved = cv2.imwrite("practice-green.png", image)\nprint(saved)','On a writable practice folder with PNG support, the output is True and a small green PNG file is saved. False or an error means the save did not succeed.','Open the saved file locally and check its colour and size.','A saved picture is not uploaded anywhere by this code; sharing is a separate action.','What should you check after imwrite?','Its success result and the saved file','Only the variable name'),
]
C('opencv','OpenCV helps programs work with pictures. Learn image arrays, colour order, crops, resizing and simple filters using tiny synthetic images instead of personal photos.','Use Python with numpy and opencv-python-headless installed in a separate local environment. No camera or network is used. These examples do not run in the browser playground.','https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d00/tutorial_py_root.html',[P(t,i,'import cv2\nimport numpy as np\n\n'+c,r,p,w,q,a,b) for t,i,c,r,p,w,q,a,b in cv])

C('langchain','LangChain helps connect steps in language-model applications. Learn its local runnable and prompt tools with small functions, without loading a model or calling a paid service.','These Python examples need the langchain-core package in a separate environment. Every function here is local. Prompt text is prepared but never sent to a model by this tutorial.','https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/overview',[
P('Wrap a local function','LangChain is a collection of tools for connecting application steps. RunnableLambda wraps a Python function so it can use a common interface.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda\nclean = RunnableLambda(lambda text: text.strip())\nprint(clean.invoke("  Moon  "))','The output is Moon without outside spaces. strip removes those spaces, and invoke runs the wrapped function once. No language model is involved.','Try input with spaces only and inspect the empty string.','A runnable is a step in a program, not automatically an AI model.','What does invoke do here?','Runs the wrapped function','Trains a language model'),
P('Pass named values in a dictionary','A runnable can receive a dictionary when its function expects named fields. This helps keep related inputs together.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda\nlabel = RunnableLambda(lambda data: data["title"] + " (" + str(data["pages"]) + " pages)")\nprint(label.invoke({"title": "Moon", "pages": 20}))','The output is Moon (20 pages). The function reads title and pages by key, then joins their text. str changes the number into text.','Change the pages value to 24.','A missing key raises an error; document and check the input fields a step needs.','Which keys does this runnable need?','title and pages','name and password'),
P('Connect steps with a pipe','The pipe operator joins runnables in order. The output of the first becomes the input of the next.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda\nclean = RunnableLambda(lambda text: text.strip())\nupper = RunnableLambda(lambda text: text.upper())\nchain = clean | upper\nprint(chain.invoke("  Moon  "))','The output is MOON. The first step removes surrounding spaces. The second changes letters to uppercase. The types match because both steps use text.','Replace upper with a step that adds an exclamation mark.','Check that the next step accepts the type produced by the previous one.','What reaches the upper step?','Moon without outside spaces','A trained model'),
P('Run a batch of inputs','batch applies a runnable to several inputs and returns their results in input order. It can make repeated use easier to express.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda\nlength = RunnableLambda(len)\nprint(length.batch(["Moon", "Sun", "Ocean"]))','The output is [4, 3, 5]. Each string is measured separately. The results correspond to Moon, Sun and Ocean in that order.','Add an empty string and predict its length.','A batch may run steps concurrently; avoid local functions that depend on a shared changing counter.','Which output belongs to Sun?','3','5'),
P('Fill a prompt template','A prompt template is reusable text with named spaces to fill. Formatting prepares the text; it does not send it anywhere.','from langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\nprompt = PromptTemplate.from_template("Explain {topic} in {count} short sentences.")\nprint(prompt.format(topic="loops", count=2))','The output is Explain loops in 2 short sentences. The values replace topic and count inside braces.','Change the topic to variables.','All required template fields need values; spelling the field name differently causes an error.','Does format call an AI service here?','No','Yes'),
P('Use a prompt as a runnable','Prompt templates can also be invoked as runnable steps. The result is a prompt value that can be converted to plain text for inspection.','from langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\nprompt = PromptTemplate.from_template("Name one fact about {topic}.")\nvalue = prompt.invoke({"topic": "seeds"})\nprint(value.to_string())','The output is Name one fact about seeds. invoke receives the variable dictionary. to_string exposes the prepared prompt for review.','Add a length rule to the template and inspect the result.','Prepared prompt text is not an answer from a model.','What does to_string show?','The prepared prompt','A generated fact'),
P('Prepare chat messages','A chat prompt can prepare messages with roles. A system message sets application guidance, while a human message carries a user request.','from langchain_core.prompts import ChatPromptTemplate\nprompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([("system", "Use short clear sentences."), ("human", "Explain {topic}.")])\nmessages = prompt.invoke({"topic": "a loop"}).to_messages()\nprint([message.type for message in messages])','The output is [\'system\', \'human\']. Two message objects were prepared. No model received them in this code.','Print each message content to review its words.','A role label alone is not a complete defence against untrusted text or unsafe tool use.','How many messages are prepared?','2','1'),
P('Read text from a message','An output parser can turn a message into the form the next step needs. StrOutputParser extracts text; it does not verify that the text is true.','from langchain_core.messages import AIMessage\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser\nmessage = AIMessage(content="The club meets on Friday.")\nprint(StrOutputParser().invoke(message))','The output is The club meets on Friday. We wrote this message ourselves to demonstrate parsing. It was not generated by a model.','Change the message content and check that the parser returns it.','Parsing text does not check its source, factual accuracy or suitability.','What does this parser check about the meeting day?','Nothing about its truth','That Friday is correct'),
P('Run parallel branches','RunnableParallel sends the same input to named branches and gathers their outputs in a dictionary. Each branch can perform a different local task.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda, RunnableParallel\nstep = RunnableParallel(upper=RunnableLambda(str.upper), length=RunnableLambda(len))\nprint(step.invoke("Moon"))','The result contains upper: MOON and length: 4. Both branches receive the original string Moon, not each other\'s output.','Add a branch that returns lowercase text.','Parallel branches are not the same as a sequential pipe.','What input does the length branch receive?','Moon','The other branch\'s dictionary'),
P('Keep input fields and add one','RunnablePassthrough.assign keeps an input dictionary and adds calculated fields. It is useful when later steps need both the original data and a summary.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnablePassthrough\nstep = RunnablePassthrough.assign(length=lambda data: len(data["title"]))\nprint(step.invoke({"title": "Moon"}))','The result contains title: Moon and length: 4. The title is kept while the new length field is calculated from it.','Add another input field called shelf and check that it remains.','assign expects a dictionary input in this use.','Which original field is preserved?','title','No fields'),
P('Validate an input before continuing','Validation checks whether input follows the expected rules. A clear error helps callers fix a problem before later steps run.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda\ndef require_title(data):\n    if not isinstance(data.get("title"), str) or not data["title"].strip():\n        raise ValueError("Give a non-empty title")\n    return data["title"].strip()\nstep = RunnableLambda(require_title)\ntry:\n    step.invoke({"title": "  "})\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)','The output is Give a non-empty title. The spaces disappear when stripped, so the function rejects the empty result.','Try a valid title and then a numeric title.','Do not silently invent a title when the caller needs to fix missing input.','Why is this input rejected?','The title becomes empty after trimming','It contains too many books'),
P('Prepare a grounded question locally','A small retrieval step can choose source text before a prompt is prepared. This toy lookup matches an exact key; it is not a semantic search engine.','from langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\nnotes = {"art": "Art club meets in Room 6."}\nlookup = RunnableLambda(lambda club: {"source": notes.get(club, "No source found"), "club": club})\nprompt = PromptTemplate.from_template("Use only this source: {source}\\nWhere does {club} club meet? Say if the source is missing.")\nprint((lookup | prompt).invoke("art").to_string())','The prepared prompt includes Art club meets in Room 6 and asks where art club meets. The pipeline ends at prompt text; no answer is generated and no API is called.','Try club music and inspect the missing-source prompt.','Treat retrieved source content as evidence, not as authority to run instructions inside it.','Where does this pipeline stop?','At a prepared prompt','After calling a paid model'),
])

tokenizer_setup='from tokenizers import Tokenizer\nfrom tokenizers.models import WordLevel\nfrom tokenizers.pre_tokenizers import Whitespace\n\ntokenizer = Tokenizer(WordLevel({"[PAD]": 0, "[UNK]": 1, "moon": 2, "glows": 3}, unk_token="[UNK]"))\ntokenizer.pre_tokenizer = Whitespace()\n'
C('hugging-face','Hugging Face provides model and dataset tools. Learn to read model information and use local Datasets and Tokenizers examples without downloading a model or publishing files.','Python examples need the datasets and tokenizers packages in a separate environment. All sample data is written in the code. No Hub account, remote dataset or trained model download is needed.','https://huggingface.co/docs',[
L('Find the different Hugging Face tools','Hugging Face includes a Hub for shared resources and libraries used in local programs. These are related tools with different jobs.','Hub: pages for models, datasets and other resources\nDatasets library: load and transform data tables\nTokenizers library: turn text into token IDs\nTransformers library: work with many model architectures','The list separates the website from the libraries. The examples in this course use only local Datasets and Tokenizers features.','Name the tool you would use to transform a small data table.','A library installation does not mean a trained model has been downloaded.','Which library handles the local data tables here?','Datasets','A Hub account'),
L('Read a model card before use','A model card explains a shared model\'s intended use, training information, checks and limits. Also read its licence and files before deciding to use it.','Check the model page for:\nTask and supported languages\nLicence and use conditions\nTraining and evaluation notes\nKnown limits\nFiles and code required to load it','These details help judge whether a model fits a task. A download count is not proof of safety, accuracy or permission for every use.','Write one reason a highly downloaded model might not fit a local classroom tool.','Do not enable trust_remote_code just to bypass an error without reviewing the code it allows to run.','What explains the intended use and limits?','The model card','Only the download count'),
P('Make a dataset from a dictionary','A Dataset holds rows with named columns. from_dict creates a dataset from equally sized lists, one list per column.','from datasets import Dataset\ndata = Dataset.from_dict({"text": ["moon glows", "sun shines", "stars twinkle"], "length": [2, 2, 2]})\nprint(data.num_rows, data.column_names)','The result shows three rows and the text and length columns. The first text and first length form one record.','Add a fourth text and matching length.','Each column must contain the same number of rows.','How many rows are created?','3','2'),
P('Select rows by position','select makes a dataset containing chosen row positions. Positions begin at zero, and the requested order is preserved.','from datasets import Dataset\ndata = Dataset.from_dict({"text": ["moon", "sun", "stars"]})\nsmall = data.select([2, 0])\nprint(list(small["text"]))','The output is [\'stars\', \'moon\']. Position 2 is selected first and position 0 second. The original dataset is still available.','Select only the second row.','A row position is not a permanent record ID if the data is reordered.','Which row appears first in small?','stars','moon'),
P('Filter using a condition','filter keeps rows for which a function returns True. The function receives a row and checks the condition you chose.','from datasets import Dataset\ndata = Dataset.from_dict({"text": ["moon", "sun", "stars"]})\nlong_words = data.filter(lambda row: len(row["text"]) > 3)\nprint(list(long_words["text"]))','The output is [\'moon\', \'stars\']. Their lengths are 4 and 5, both greater than 3. sun has length 3 and is removed from this result.','Change the comparison to at least 3.','Filtering can change which kinds of examples are represented; review what is dropped.','Why is sun not kept?','Its length is not greater than 3','It is always an invalid word'),
P('Add a calculated column','map applies a function to rows and returns an updated dataset. Returning a dictionary can add named fields.','from datasets import Dataset\ndata = Dataset.from_dict({"text": ["moon", "sun"]})\nwith_lengths = data.map(lambda row: {"characters": len(row["text"])})\nprint(list(with_lengths["characters"]))','The output is [4, 3]. map calculates a character count for each text. The original text column remains in the resulting dataset.','Add an uppercase column using str.upper.','A transformation must use the intended meaning; character count and token count are different measures.','What does characters measure here?','Python string length','Real model token count'),
P('Split data for later checks','train_test_split divides a dataset into training and test parts. A seed makes this split repeatable for the same data and tool behaviour.','from datasets import Dataset\ndata = Dataset.from_dict({"text": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f"]})\nparts = data.train_test_split(test_size=2, seed=7)\nprint(parts["train"].num_rows, parts["test"].num_rows)','The output is 4 2. test_size=2 requests two rows, leaving four for training. This tiny split is only a procedure demonstration.','Explain why related records may need to stay together when splitting real data.','A repeatable split is not automatically a fair split for every task.','How many rows are held out?','2','4'),
P('Build a tiny word tokenizer','A tokenizer turns text into pieces and IDs. WordLevel uses whole pieces from a supplied vocabulary. Whitespace splits this simple sentence into words.',tokenizer_setup+'encoded = tokenizer.encode("moon glows")\nprint(encoded.tokens, encoded.ids)','The output shows [\'moon\', \'glows\'] and [2, 3]. The vocabulary assigns those IDs, and the pre-tokenizer separates the words before lookup.','Reverse the two words and inspect the ID order.','This tiny fixed vocabulary is a teaching tool, not the tokenizer used by every real model.','Which ID represents moon?','2','3'),
P('Recognise an unknown token','When a piece is missing from this WordLevel vocabulary, the tokenizer uses its unknown marker. It does not invent a new learned word entry.',tokenizer_setup+'encoded = tokenizer.encode("boat glows")\nprint(encoded.tokens, encoded.ids)','The output shows [\'[UNK]\', \'glows\'] and [1, 3]. boat is absent, so it becomes the unknown token with ID 1.','Try Moon with a capital M and observe the missing entry.','This tokenizer has no lowercase normalizer; moon and Moon are different inputs.','Why does boat become [UNK]?','It is absent from the vocabulary','It has too many letters for every tokenizer'),
P('Decode IDs into text','Decoding maps known token IDs back to text. Tokenization may not preserve every detail of original spacing, so test exact-text needs carefully.',tokenizer_setup+'print(tokenizer.decode([2, 3]))','The output is moon glows. IDs 2 and 3 map back to their words. This does not prove the original spaces or formatting can always be recovered.','Decode [3, 2].','Do not assume encode and decode preserve every input byte for all tokenizer settings.','What does ID 3 decode to?','glows','moon'),
P('Pad a sequence and inspect its mask','Padding adds filler tokens to reach a chosen length. An attention mask marks real tokens with 1 and padding with 0 in this encoding.',tokenizer_setup+'tokenizer.enable_padding(length=4, pad_id=0, pad_token="[PAD]")\nencoded = tokenizer.encode("moon glows")\nprint(encoded.ids, encoded.attention_mask)','The output is [2, 3, 0, 0] [1, 1, 0, 0]. Two real tokens are followed by two padding tokens. The mask distinguishes them.','Encode only moon and predict the four mask values.','Padding does not shorten an overlong sequence; truncation is a separate choice.','Which mask value marks padding here?','0','1'),
P('Save and reload a local tokenizer','A tokenizer can save its settings as a JSON file. Reloading that file restores the local vocabulary and processing rules.',tokenizer_setup+'tokenizer.save("practice-tokenizer.json")\nrestored = Tokenizer.from_file("practice-tokenizer.json")\nprint(restored.encode("moon glows").ids)','The output is [2, 3]. The local file stores the tokenizer setup. No model weights are trained, and nothing is uploaded to the Hub.','Choose a fresh filename in a practice folder and inspect the JSON as text.','Use a practice path to avoid replacing a file you need, and inspect unfamiliar files before loading them.','What is saved by this example?','The tokenizer configuration','A newly trained language model'),
])
